jurnal.uibu.ac.id", "url": "https://ejurnal.uibu.ac.id/index.php/maharsi/article/download/2558/1762/8742"}, {"title": "- Global Voices - https://globalvoices.org -", "url": "https://globalvoices.org/2015/11/15/toraja-festival-features-traditional-dances-of-indonesia/print/"}, {"title": "journal.unesa.ac.id", "url": "https://journal.unesa.ac.id/index.php/Repertoar/article/download/40230/13628"}]}, "I didn't find a Wikipedia page on the Ma'bua' dance in these results. The only source for the 12-year figure is a travel article, and it's secondhand.\n\n- **The 12-year claim:** A Jakarta Now travel article quotes a local guide describing a Ma'bua dance in which a priest wears a buffalo head and dances around a sacred tree, held only once every 12 years. This is a single anecdotal source, not an authoritative one.\n- **What Ma'bua is:** A journal article on Pa'gellu' describes ma'bua as an inauguration ceremony for a Tongkonan house, one of the traditional Toraja houses. It notes that Pa'gellu' is often performed at ma'bua and other celebrations.\n- **Ceremonial context:** An Indonesian-language paper lists ma'bua among the thanksgiving feasts (rambu tuka') held for things like completing a traditional house, weddings, and harvests.\n\nI'd treat the 12-year interval as unverified. A Wikipedia article or a Toraja cultural-studies source would be needed to confirm it."], "durationSeconds": 5.447121374999996, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01K651oiZhFrvpLstXNP1CxQ", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
